    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d583">

                <pb facs="0749" n="737" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg737"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.24/1234b: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Second Secretary of
                        Embassy in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Thompson</hi>)<anchor n="28" xml:id="d583-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-01-07T07:00:00-04:00">January 7, 1943—7
                            a.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>12. From Stettinius<anchor n="29" xml:id="d583-f2"/> to Faymonville.<anchor
                        n="30" xml:id="d583-f3"/> We have informed Litvinov<anchor n="31"
                        xml:id="d583-f4"/> that the time is at hand to initiate the Third Protocol.
                    Soviet requirements for the period July 1, 1943 to June 30, 1944 are desired.
                    Presentation of these should be grouped to correspond as nearly as <pb
                        facs="0750" n="738" xml:id="pg738"/> possible to the Second Protocol
                    categories. Planning here will be aided by promptness in as much as production
                    schedules are now being set up well in advance. Alloy steel requirements are
                    especially urgent. [Stettinius.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d583-f1" n="28" type="source" xml:id="d583-f1-content">
                <p>Llewellyn E. Thompson, Jr., Second Secretary of Embassy and Consul in the Soviet
                    Union, was in charge of the group remaining in Moscow after the removal of the
                    Embassy to Kuibyshev.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f2" n="29" xml:id="d583-f2-content">
                <p>Edward R. Stettinius, Jr., Lend-Lease Administrator.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f3" n="30" xml:id="d583-f3-content">
                <p>Brig. Gen. Philip R. Faymonville, head of the United States Supply Mission in the
                    Soviet Union; Lend-Lease Representative.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d583-f4" n="31" xml:id="d583-f4-content">
                <p>Maxim Maximovich Litvinov, Soviet Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
